#====================================================================
# DRAFT-asmc-native-arm64-host-x86-64-elf-ldlld.kv
# ARM64 OHOS 原生 host 汇编器输出 x86/x86-64 目标并链接真实 EXEC ELF
# Draft only. Do not promote into knowledge/graph until promoted.
#====================================================================

id:                     DRAFT-asmc-native-arm64-host-x86-64-elf-ldlld
trigger_tool:           clang|make|asmc|ld.lld|conan
phase:                  building|linking|targeting|packaging
error_signature:        cannot find entry symbol|elf_x86_64|non-local symbol.*sh_info|
                         GLOBAL.*STB_LOCAL|unsupported emulation|no active task|no elf_x86_64
severity:               3
category:               toolchain|aarch64|linker|elf|x86-64|cross-target
match_keywords:         ARM64, aarch64, OHOS, native host, asmc, x86-64, x86_64, elf_x86_64,
                         ld.lld, GNU ld, executable ELF, locals-first, STB_GLOBAL, symbol table,
                         cross-target, assembler, JWasm
title:                  Native ARM64 host may assemble x86/x86-64 target and link a real EXEC ELF with ld.lld
root_cause:             The host runs on aarch64 OHOS (clang 15.0.4, GNU ld with only
                         aarch64/arm emulations), so GNU ld cannot emit elf_x86_64. The
                         assembler emitted every symbol as STB_LOCAL and .symtab was not
                         locals-first, so the linker could not resolve an entry symbol.
                         The fix is: emit proc symbols as STB_GLOBAL, lay .symtab
                         locals-first with correct sh_info, and select the already-present
                         LLD 15.0.4 linker (-m elf_x86_64) which is host-independent.
affected_scope:         Any tool that compiles/assembles on aarch64 OHOS and must emit
                         x86/x86-64 target ELF objects and/or an x86-64 executable ELF
                         (assemblers, JIT-compiled helpers, cross-linkable toolchains).
verification_cmd:       ld.lld -m elf_x86_64 -static -e main -o t.elf t.o; readelf -h t.elf

rec_priority:           1
rec_approach:           arch-separation-and-ldlld
rec_description:        Keep host architecture (aarch64) separate from target architecture
                         (x86-64). Repair the assembler ELF writer: emit `name proc` symbols
                         as STB_GLOBAL, sort .symtab locals-first then globals with sh_info =
                         1 + nloc, and remap relocation symbol indices. Then link with the
                         already-present ld.lld 15.0.4 (-m elf_x86_64 -static -e main) to
                         produce a real x86-64 executable ELF. Do NOT use QEMU/Wine/fixed ELF/stub.
rec_command:            make rebuild;
                         ld.lld -m elf_x86_64 -static -e main -o r209_evidence/e2d_x64_exec.elf \
                           r209_evidence/e2d_x64prog.o;
                         readelf -h r209_evidence/e2d_x64_exec.elf
rec_auto_fix:          true
rec_impl:               batch-command

first_error:            earliest reproducible blocker is GNU ld on aarch64 OHOS: `ld:
                         unsupported emulation 'elf_x86_64'` (GNU ld only ships
                         aarch64/arm emulations; no elf_x86_64 available).
diagnose_order:         1) `make rebuild` on native ARM64 host — do NOT use emulation;
                         2) inspect the produced object with `readelf -h`/`-s`: confirm
                         ELF64 x86-64 REL (.o) and check .symtab ordering + bind types;
                         3) if `ld`/GNU ld is the linker and it fails with unsupported
                         emulation elf_x86_64, switch to ld.lld 15.0.4 (`ld.lld --help`
                         shows target emulations; it is host-independent);
                         4) link `-m elf_x86_64 -static -e main`; 5) verify with
                         `readelf -h` that E_TYPE = EXEC (x86-64) and Entry is non-zero;
                         REL (.o) is NOT an executable — a REL object alone is not a pass.
forbid_1_pattern:       using QEMU/Wine/emulators or fixed ELF bytes or stub executable to fake the target
forbid_1_reason:        that does not demonstrate a real native host -> x86/x86-64 target chain
forbid_1_consequence:   evidence would be rejected; does not validate the actual cross-target pipeline
forbid_2_pattern:       reporting an x86/x86-64 REL object (.o) as an executable ELF, or
                         claiming EXEC/run without `readelf -h` E_TYPE proof
forbid_2_reason:        REL is a relocatable object, not a runnable program; an object
                         alone cannot demonstrate the cross-target executable pipeline
forbid_2_consequence:   misleading evidence; will not pass W3/finish audit
fallback_priority:      2
fallback_approach:      consult-knowledge
fallback_description:   If ld.lld is unavailable, inventory all local linkers and report the
                         earliest reproducible blocker with exact target/emulation support.
fallback_command:       ld -V; ld.lld --help; clang --print-targets
fallback_auto_fix:     false
fallback_impl:          none
principles:              P016
caused_by:               
similar_to:             E050, E085
conflicts_with:               
prerequisite_of:        

